#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539035} :activity/id #uuid "0e4eb3c8-3084-59ce-b3fc-2b8b5311bf40" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539035 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538983 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0174e176-0cb7-5b47-a8ba-9fe3b08757a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538238} {:answer/id #uuid "4f2d612f-1d4f-54a0-829f-81ec557baecc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538402} {:answer/id #uuid "53c58a6b-8263-5f64-84ba-2acd0232972e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538412} {:answer/id #uuid "623914d6-e6b8-5dc3-8327-81f1d2919a84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538452} {:answer/id #uuid "fce51717-00cf-5470-b72d-97e68246b052" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538802}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538452} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ce54675-4c82-511c-b17c-ef8ba86365cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538855}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "9820b99c-2acb-5f80-b231-9670a302c372" :question/math-academy-id "q-37402" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3$ of the matrix $A,$ given above. Find the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[a,\\,b,\\,c]^{T}$ such that the set $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent. What is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find all the eigenvalues of $A$ by solving the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda  & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-(2)(2)) & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ -(\\lambda -2)(\\lambda +1)(\\lambda -3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$ Since we obtained three distinct eigenvalues, the set of eigenvectors $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\},$ where each vector corresponds to different eigenvalue, will be linearly independent.\n\nNow, recall that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3.$ Thus, the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ must correspond to ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$ \n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =-1.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]-(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{3}{8}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{-1}{1}=-1.$"} :step/id #uuid "0f789298-4a09-5af7-941a-c1ea2e019531" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539041}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539041 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538963 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4659c64c-5190-52e8-8db5-eb121e204831" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538377} {:answer/id #uuid "47e6eaaf-0488-5e0a-b6b7-4666b416074b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538383} {:answer/id #uuid "6e14df0f-9ce2-5965-bb91-1fbe22d21aa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538479} {:answer/id #uuid "965fd1e1-14aa-51e2-add1-57ed1e1880f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538586} {:answer/id #uuid "bb3ee915-a0f5-5e10-b5c6-cadf0c09fd1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538672}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538672} :answer-field/id #uuid "f81fd95e-548c-5041-a689-fc8dd1a4f6b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538918}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "629a07aa-1bdb-505b-9cdf-8118c6579d3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-136955" :question/problem "Find $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}],$ given that the continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{x+2}{8},\\,-2\\le x\\le 2.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a continuous random variable $X$ and a function $r(x),$ the rule of the lazy statistician states that \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X)]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }r(x)f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\nr(x)={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }r(x)f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{x+2}{8}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{8}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{32}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{12}]}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{16}{32}+\\frac{8}{12}]-[\\frac{16}{32}-\\frac{8}{12}] \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1a19aacf-ab2d-5dcb-813b-8690528eee79" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539113}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539049 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538935 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "440832e3-ec7e-581c-830b-fa0617454e21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538370} {:answer/id #uuid "582dfc2f-ec43-56ae-b0da-a85459f5f647" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538419} {:answer/id #uuid "6ce9db67-a4d8-528a-ab5f-3678c39d003b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538477} {:answer/id #uuid "8f9bda09-f1d7-582f-8b9e-ab2ac1effc46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538570} {:answer/id #uuid "cf97a1ce-c65c-5f65-82ac-6f5c300dc9ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538713}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538713} :answer-field/id #uuid "30f439c6-850c-556a-a1db-f9a9d3a4a19c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538829}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "224b72f4-7dda-5cd7-89ac-9950a44cc628" :question/math-academy-id "q-132437" :question/problem "Given that $X∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}),$ what is $\\text{Var}[X]?$" :question/worked-solution "If $X∼\\text{Exp}(\\lambda )$ is an exponential random variable, then\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\frac{1}{{\\lambda }^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for our random variable $X∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}),$ we have the following variance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\frac{1}{{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{3}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "28a75cf2-cb50-5c8b-8c4a-1129de8d5e56"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539080 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338997 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "440337ae-e75b-5e2e-af72-f244faaa1466" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467884} {:answer/id #uuid "84120805-e8ea-59f8-8390-7f0edacbe1f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467885}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186467885} :answer-field/id #uuid "df2c75d9-b4ef-5178-ba10-74ba6baa1d94" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468663} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "afd201de-1dae-5ce1-9c43-571f88d6eff0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467886} {:answer/id #uuid "bb427474-e6dd-5ecd-9ad5-db3243527e40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467887}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186467886} :answer-field/id #uuid "a36383fc-6ca1-5ff1-8d63-ce4e0dfeba1b" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468664} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "703aafea-bae6-565a-8269-49fb586e4b48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467888} {:answer/id #uuid "e33ef251-1fda-59d8-80c6-61a0acfd6e0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467889}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186467889} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b47c5fa-a986-5f5a-a620-2cf86189fd7c" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468665}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ef298213-bf03-538b-9273-2913d7465266" :question/math-academy-id "q-71819" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are neither open nor closed?\n\nI. $\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 3,\\,0<{y}^{2}\\le 2\\}$\n\nII. $\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0<{x}^{2}<9,\\,1<{y}^{2}<4\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0\\le x<1,\\,{y}^{2}<4\\}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *neither open nor closed*. It contains some of its boundary points, for example, the point $(1,\\sqrt{2}).$ On the other hand, it does not contain some boundary points, for example, the point $(1,0).$\n\n- The second set is *open* since it doesn't contain any point of its boundary (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The third set is *neither open nor closed*. It contains some of its boundary points, for example, the point $(0,0).$ On the other hand, it does not contain some other boundary points, for example, the point $(0,2).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "9374cbd9-c90e-598d-b18c-d1e10e57c7d1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539049}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539113 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311742 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "057a87a0-f2e0-5ded-9e5b-e55e988beb91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308790} {:answer/id #uuid "0eb353c6-55f3-5856-b487-88af00f0c776" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308849} {:answer/id #uuid "ceea24a6-1169-5dfc-96b2-cc63f36822e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310410} {:answer/id #uuid "de4bd802-c89d-5919-bee4-6cbe13e84831" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310531} {:answer/id #uuid "e6d5c2bc-9894-547b-9710-238479ebe005" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310582}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308849} :answer-field/id #uuid "66524e78-f254-500a-8d22-7a05143b8c39" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311061}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7180d4ef-44aa-537f-8256-f62f77cfe5b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-336740" :question/problem "![](images/7f/7f997f7647886e1eea24d292ddd66b2d331d6f7d4c7ffe47b6a52b52dd029a44.png)\n\nConsider the RL circuit shown above. Given that the current $I(t)$ at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\nI(t)=A{e}^{-6t}+\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A,$ $\\alpha ,$ and $\\beta $ are constants, find the value of $\\alpha +\\beta .$" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $12\\,Ω,$ and an inductor of inductance $2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=12\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=12I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=2\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)=12I+2\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+6I=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+6{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-6t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $3t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $3t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))+6(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ (-3\\alpha +6\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+(6\\alpha +3\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3\\alpha +6\\beta &=2\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\ 6\\alpha +3\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{2}{15},$ $\\beta =\\frac{4}{15}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{2}{15}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{4}{15}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-6t}-\\frac{2}{15}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{4}{15}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =-\\frac{2}{15},\\,\\beta =\\frac{4}{15}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha +\\beta  & =-\\frac{2}{15}+\\frac{4}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ef437e04-1455-5db2-bea2-dccf36db5aaf" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539080}}] :activity/title "Quiz 2" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045460 :db/ident :activity.type/assessment} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538217}]}
